"""Accuracy cases for the functional ``aten::_foreach_log2`` operator.

Coverage source is the frozen master family file ``tests/test_foreach_unary.py``
of the same checkout, restricted to the rows that master's own
``pytest.mark.foreach_log2`` marker selects (``_op_params`` attaches that marker
per parameter, master lines 54-68). This file replaces the frozen dev file
``tests/test_foreach_log2.py``, whose workloads are not master's: float inputs
``(4, 7)``/``(3, 5)``, a single ``int32`` list of shape ``(11,)`` and a complex
list of ``(9,)`` instead of the family's own dtype x shape grid.

Transported rows (master line numbers in ``tests/test_foreach_unary.py``):

* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_float[log2-float16|float32|bfloat16]``
  (245-256): three tensors of shape ``(16, 8)``, ``_sample`` range
  ``rand * 0.8 + 0.1``, ``to_reference`` without upcast, ATen parity.
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_int[log2-int16|int32|int64]`` (259-274):
  three tensors of shape ``(32,)``, ``randint(1, 5)``; the promotion policy is
  asserted through dtype equality with ATen (``int_to_float`` for ``log2``).
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_bool[log2]`` (277-292): one
  ``[True, False, True]`` tensor. The ``abs``-only CPU workaround in that body
  does not apply to ``log2``.
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_complex[log2-complex64]`` (295-307): two
  complex tensors of shape ``(16,)``, gated on the extracted ``complex_fn``
  being present, which it is for ``log2``.
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_complex_large[log2-complex64|complex128]``
  (656-671): the modulus-overflow row; ``log2`` is in ``MODULUS_OPS``
  (master 653) and ``LARGE_COMPLEX`` is master's ``1e20`` / ``1e200`` map
  (master 647-650).

Assertions kept from master: ``_assert_lists_close`` (length, per-tensor dtype,
``gems_assert_close`` for floating/complex and ``gems_assert_equal`` otherwise)
and, from the frozen Definition ``definitions/foreach_log2.json``
(``effects.returns_alias_of == {}``), the dev file's own non-aliasing assertion
on every out-of-place result. The dev file's ``RuntimeError`` on an empty list
is a frozen rejection clause and is kept.

Per-operator metadata is extracted, not imported. Master's table row lives in
``src/flag_gems/ops/_foreach_unary.py``: ``UNARY_OPS`` is imported at
``tests/test_foreach_unary.py:24``, but the frozen dev tree has no
``flag_gems/ops/_foreach_unary.py`` at all, so that import can only fail
collection (``ImportError`` -> probe ``API_UNAVAILABLE``). The exact row for
``log2`` (master 655-662) and the dtype sets it references (master 535-555) are
therefore restated below verbatim in meaning, with no re-derivation:

    UnaryOp("log2", _scalar_fn(log2_func), int_to_float, NO_COMPLEX32, c_log2,
            fn16=_fast_log2_func)

Callable names: master's ``_gems_op`` resolves ``flag_gems._foreach_log2``
(master 89-95). This version's Definition names the operator ``foreach_log2``
and the formal override key ``foreach_log2`` assigns that module attribute
(``dynamic_registry.DynamicOpOverride.override``, ``setattr(module, op_name,
impl_func)``), which is also the name the frozen dev tests and Definition use,
so the attribute is looked up by that name at call time. The ATen baseline uses
master's own ``torch._foreach_log2`` spelling.

Master rows that are NOT transported, kept here as explicit unresolved scope
rather than silently pruned or converted into skips:

* ``test_operator_markers_are_declared`` (master 180-243): a static-discovery
  placeholder that executes nothing (metadata test).
* ``test_foreach_unary_registration_is_live`` (master 507-542) and the three
  ``test_foreach_unary_launch_count_*`` tests (master 561-632): they carry every
  unary marker, but each returns before touching the operator unless
  ``flag_gems.device == "cuda"`` (master 517-518, 570-571, 600-601, 624-625), so
  on this ``npu`` backend they can only pass without invoking the candidate.
  They exercise ``caplog``/Triton launch counting and CUDA device events
  (master 545-558); neither is transportable here and neither may be turned
  into a skip that pretends to cover them.
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_rejects_unsupported_dtype[log2]`` (master
  310-322): ``log2``'s ``allowed`` set contains all three probe dtypes
  (``bool``, ``int32``, ``complex64``), so the body is empty for this operator.
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_out_overload`` (master 489-504): its parameters
  are ``REPRESENTATIVE`` (``neg``/``sin``/``abs``/``round``), so the ``log2``
  marker does not select it; the ``.out`` overload registration gap stays
  unresolved and uncovered here.
* ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_list_lengths`` / ``_mixed_shapes`` /
  ``_empty_and_zero_dim`` / ``_mixed_dtypes`` / ``_layouts`` (master 325-414)
  and ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_error_parity`` (master 479-486): all
  parameterized over ``REPRESENTATIVE``, so no ``log2`` rows exist there.
* The in-place entry points (master 417-476) belong to the separate operator id
  ``foreach_log2_`` (``definitions/foreach_log2_.json``) and are out of scope
  for this task.
* Dev-only extra workloads are not carried over: the transposed
  ``(4, 7)``/``(3, 5)`` rows, the ``int32`` ``(11,)`` row, the mixed-shape row
  with an empty tensor and the ``(9,)`` complex row. Workload is frozen to
  master, and none of those rows has a master counterpart for ``log2``. The
  consequence, stated rather than hidden, is that this file has no
  non-contiguous / gappy-view / storage-offset case for ``log2`` at all: in
  master those live in the ``REPRESENTATIVE``-only ``_layouts`` row.
* The complex rows (``test_accuracy_foreach_log2_complex`` and both
  ``test_accuracy_foreach_log2_complex_large`` parameters) are SKIPPED on this
  Ascend backend, and only there, because the native reference they need cannot
  produce a value: ``torch._foreach_log2`` raises ``RuntimeError: input must be
  half, float or bfloat16`` for ``complex64``/``complex128`` _before_ the
  candidate is called. This is measured, not assumed: in
  ``review/artifacts/accuracy.log:4-59`` the traceback stops at
  ``ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)`` and ``review/artifacts/accuracy-native.json``
  records those three rows with ``candidate_calls: {flag_gems.foreach_log2: 0}``.
  The rows stay in the file and stay selected by ``-m foreach_log2``, and are
  reported as skipped: not deleted, not cast to a real dtype, not compared
  against an invented CPU oracle, and no numerical tolerance is touched. Every
  float, int and bool row keeps its original assertions. No other vendor's
  complex behaviour is inferred from this one measurement.
"""

import pytest
import torch

import flag_gems

from . import accuracy_utils as utils

# --- extracted per-op metadata (master src/flag_gems/ops/_foreach_unary.py) ---
# master 539-555, the dtype sets the table rows reference.
_FLOAT = (torch.float16, torch.bfloat16, torch.float32, torch.float64)
_INT = (torch.int8, torch.int16, torch.int32, torch.int64, torch.uint8)
# master 553-555: NO_COMPLEX32 == _FLOAT + _INT + (bool, complex64, complex128).
NO_COMPLEX32 = frozenset(
    _FLOAT + _INT + (torch.bool, torch.complex64, torch.complex128)
)

_FAMILY = "log2"
# master 655-662: the log2 row of UNARY_OPS.
_ROW_ALLOWED = NO_COMPLEX32
_ROW_COMPLEX_FN = True  # the row passes c_log2, not None
_ROW_OUT_DTYPE_INT_TO_FLOAT = True  # out_dtype_fn=int_to_float
_ROW_HAS_FAST_FN16 = True  # fn16=_fast_log2_func

# master 647-650: magnitudes whose modulus is representable but whose square is
# not, so a naive component-dtype modulus overflows.
_LARGE_COMPLEX = {
    torch.complex64: 1e20,
    torch.complex128: 1e200,
}


def _supports(dtype):
    """master ``_supports("log2", dtype)`` (master 118-125).

    ``IS_KUNLUNXIN`` is False for this backend, so the vendor tables do not
    apply and only the row's own ``allowed`` set decides.
    """
    return _ROW_ALLOWED is None or dtype in _ROW_ALLOWED


# Measured Ascend capability limit of the reference side, not of the candidate:
# the device-side ATen op rejects complex inputs for this operator, so there is
# no native value to compare against. Evidence (reference-as-solution run):
# ``review/artifacts/accuracy.log:4-59`` raises at
# ``ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)`` with "RuntimeError: input must be half,
# float or bfloat16", and ``review/artifacts/accuracy-native.json`` records the
# same three rows with ``candidate_calls: {flag_gems.foreach_log2: 0}``.
# ``utils.TO_CPU`` is the dev suite's device/CPU reference switch: when the
# reference runs on CPU ("--ref cpu") the device limit does not apply.
_NATIVE_REFERENCE_REJECTS_COMPLEX = (
    flag_gems.vendor_name == "ascend" and not utils.TO_CPU
)


def _require_native_reference(dtype):
    """Skip a single row whose reference value this backend cannot produce.

    Scope is deliberately narrow: complex dtypes only, on the one backend where
    the rejection above was actually observed, and only while the reference runs
    on the device. Float, int and bool rows never reach the skip.
    """
    if dtype.is_complex and _NATIVE_REFERENCE_REJECTS_COMPLEX:
        pytest.skip(
            f"no native reference on ascend for {dtype}: torch._foreach_log2 "
            "raises 'RuntimeError: input must be half, float or bfloat16' "
            "before the candidate is called (candidate_calls == 0); evidence "
            "repair-v23-binding-review/tasks/ascend-1831/review/artifacts/"
            "accuracy.log and .../accuracy-native.json"
        )


def _sample(shape, dtype, device):
    """master ``_sample(shape, dtype, device, name="log2")`` (master 134-152)."""
    if dtype.is_floating_point:
        return torch.rand(shape, dtype=dtype, device=device) * 0.8 + 0.1
    if dtype.is_complex:
        real = torch.rand(shape, dtype=torch.float32, device=device) * 0.8 + 0.1
        imag = torch.rand(shape, dtype=torch.float32, device=device) * 0.8 + 0.1
        return torch.complex(real, imag).to(dtype)
    if dtype is torch.bool:
        return torch.randint(0, 2, shape, device=device).to(torch.bool)
    # "log2" is not in master's UNIT_DOMAIN (master 131), so the integral range
    # stays [1, 5).
    return torch.randint(1, 5, shape, dtype=dtype, device=device)


def _torch_op():
    """master ``_torch_op("log2")``: ``torch._foreach_log2`` (master 85-86)."""
    return getattr(torch, f"_foreach_{_FAMILY}")


def _gems_op():
    """The export the formal ``--override foreach_log2:...:run`` target sets."""
    return getattr(flag_gems, f"foreach_{_FAMILY}")


def _assert_lists_close(res, ref, name=_FAMILY):
    """master ``_assert_lists_close`` (master 155-162), plus shape parity.

    The shape assertion is the dev file's ``_assert_values`` check
    (dev ``tests/test_foreach_log2.py:76``); it tightens nothing that master's
    parity call does not already imply, and it is kept so the replacement does
    not drop a check the executed suite had.
    """
    assert len(res) == len(ref)
    for got, want in zip(res, ref):
        assert got.shape == want.shape
        assert got.dtype == want.dtype, f"{name}: {got.dtype} != {want.dtype}"
        if got.dtype.is_floating_point or got.dtype.is_complex:
            utils.gems_assert_close(got, want, got.dtype)
        else:
            utils.gems_assert_equal(got, want)


def _assert_no_alias(values, result):
    """Frozen Definition clause ``effects.returns_alias_of == {}``.

    ``definitions/foreach_log2.json:19``; the same assertion the dev file made
    (dev ``tests/test_foreach_log2.py:100-103``).
    """
    for output, source in zip(result, values):
        assert (
            output.untyped_storage().data_ptr()
            != source.untyped_storage().data_ptr()
        ), "result aliases its input storage; Definition forbids aliasing"


@pytest.mark.foreach_log2
@pytest.mark.parametrize("dtype", [torch.float16, torch.float32, torch.bfloat16])
def test_accuracy_foreach_log2_float(dtype):
    """master ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_float[log2-*]`` (master 245-256)."""
    assert _supports(dtype)
    inp = [_sample((16, 8), dtype, flag_gems.device) for _ in range(3)]
    ref_inp = [utils.to_reference(t) for t in inp]

    ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)
    res_out = _gems_op()(inp)

    _assert_lists_close(res_out, ref_out)
    _assert_no_alias(inp, res_out)


@pytest.mark.foreach_log2
@pytest.mark.parametrize("dtype", [torch.int16, torch.int32, torch.int64])
def test_accuracy_foreach_log2_int(dtype):
    """master ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_int[log2-*]`` (master 259-274).

    The promotion policy is part of the contract: a transcendental returns
    float32 for an integral input. It is asserted through the dtype equality
    with the ATen result, exactly as master does, not through a hardcoded
    target dtype.
    """
    assert _supports(dtype)
    assert _ROW_OUT_DTYPE_INT_TO_FLOAT
    inp = [_sample((32,), dtype, flag_gems.device) for _ in range(3)]
    ref_inp = [utils.to_reference(t) for t in inp]

    ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)
    res_out = _gems_op()(inp)

    _assert_lists_close(res_out, ref_out)
    _assert_no_alias(inp, res_out)


@pytest.mark.foreach_log2
def test_accuracy_foreach_log2_bool():
    """master ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_bool[log2]`` (master 277-292)."""
    assert _supports(torch.bool)
    inp = [torch.tensor([True, False, True], device=flag_gems.device)]
    ref_inp = [utils.to_reference(t) for t in inp]

    ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)
    res_out = _gems_op()(inp)

    _assert_lists_close(res_out, ref_out)
    _assert_no_alias(inp, res_out)


@pytest.mark.foreach_log2
def test_accuracy_foreach_log2_complex():
    """master ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_complex[log2-complex64]`` (295-307)."""
    dtype = torch.complex64
    assert _supports(dtype)
    assert _ROW_COMPLEX_FN
    _require_native_reference(dtype)
    inp = [_sample((16,), dtype, flag_gems.device) for _ in range(2)]
    ref_inp = [utils.to_reference(t) for t in inp]

    ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)
    res_out = _gems_op()(inp)

    _assert_lists_close(res_out, ref_out)
    _assert_no_alias(inp, res_out)


@pytest.mark.foreach_log2
@pytest.mark.parametrize("dtype", [torch.complex64, torch.complex128])
def test_accuracy_foreach_log2_complex_large(dtype):
    """master ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_complex_large[log2-*]`` (656-671)."""
    assert _supports(dtype)
    assert _ROW_COMPLEX_FN
    _require_native_reference(dtype)
    mag = _LARGE_COMPLEX[dtype]
    base = torch.tensor([complex(mag, mag), complex(mag, -mag)], dtype=dtype)
    inp = [base.to(flag_gems.device)]
    ref_inp = [utils.to_reference(t) for t in inp]

    ref_out = _torch_op()(ref_inp)
    res_out = _gems_op()(inp)

    _assert_lists_close(res_out, ref_out)
    _assert_no_alias(inp, res_out)


@pytest.mark.foreach_log2
def test_foreach_log2_rejects_empty_list():
    """Frozen rejection clause: an empty TensorList raises ``RuntimeError``.

    Kept from the dev file (dev ``tests/test_foreach_log2.py:152-155``). Master
    states the same clause inside ``test_accuracy_foreach_unary_error_parity``
    (master 479-486), whose parameters are ``REPRESENTATIVE``-only, so the
    ``log2`` marker does not select it there.
    """
    with pytest.raises(RuntimeError):
        _gems_op()([])
